rs in hand, plus the two melds are not pairs, so that doesn't help.\n3. Other special patterns unlikely.\n\nSo I need to form two more melds from T3, T8, B2, B4, F2. Let's see possibilities:\n\n- T3: can form chow with T1T2 or T2T4 or T4T5? But T4T5T6 is already a meld, so T4 is used. T3 could form chow with T1T2 or T2T4? But T2 is available? Unshown tiles: T2: 3 left, T1: 2 left. Possibly.\n- T8: can form chow with T6T7 or T7T9 or T9? T6 is used in meld, T7 available (4 left), T9 (1 left). So T8 could form T6T7T8 or T7T8T9.\n- B2: can form chow with B1B3 or B3B4? B4 is in hand. B3 available (3 left). B1 (3 left). Also pung with two more B2 (4 left? Unshown B2: 4, but I have 2, so 2 left).\n- B4: chow with B2B3 or B3B5 or B5B6? B2 in hand. B3 (3 left), B5 (3 left). Also pung with two more B4 (unshown B4: 4, I have 2, so 2 left).\n- F2: pung with two more F2 (unshown F2: 4, I have 2, so 2 left). No chow for honors.\n\nSo possible melds: \n- Chow: T1T2T3, T2T3T4, T3T4T5 (but T4T5 used), T6T7T8, T7T8T9\n- Chow: B1B2B3, B2B3B4, B3B4B5, B4B5B6\n- Pung: B2 pung, B4 pung, F2 pung.\n\nGiven I have pairs of B2, B4, F2, turning one into a pung would require one more tile each, and then I'd have a pair left from another set. For example, if I get a third B2, I have B2 pung, then pair B4 and pair F2, plus T3 and T8 need to form a chow together? They are not consecutive. So I'd need two chows from T3 and T8, but they are different numbers and not close. Alternatively, I could aim for a hand with two pungs and one chow, using T3 and T8 to form a chow with a drawn tile? But T3 and T8 are far apart, cannot form a chow together. So likely I need to discard either T3 or T8 to reduce the number of singletons, and try to form pungs/chows with the pairs.\n\nIf I discard T3, then I have: T8, B2 pair, B4 pair, F2 pair. Need two melds from T8, B2, B4, F2. Possibilities: \n- Get T6 or T9 to form chow with T7T8 or T8T9? Actually T8 chow requires T6T7T8 or T7T8T9. So need T6 and T7, or T7 and