8, F2. Among these, which is safest? Looking at game history:  \n- Opponents have discarded some tiles:  \n  Previous player discarded B6, B9, B1, B7, etc.  \n  Next player discarded B8, B3.  \n  Across discarded B8.  \nSo B8 has been discarded twice (by next and across), so only 1 B8 left in wall (since total 4, 2 discarded, 1 in unknown? Actually unshown B8:1 means 1 left not seen, so yes only 1 left). B7 discarded by previous, unshown B7:2 left. B9 discarded by me earlier and by previous, unshown B9:2 left. So B9 is not rare.  \n\nI need to consider defense: discard safe tiles to avoid giving others pung/kong. Look at opponents' melds:  \n- Across has a T1-2-3 chow, so they might need T suits.  \n- Previous has W6-7-8 chow, so they might need W suits.  \n- Next has no melds.  \n\nDiscarding T3 might be riskier because across has T1-2-3, so they might need T3 for pung? But they already have a chow of T1-2-3, so they might not need T3 for pung unless they have another T3. Unshown T3:3 left, so possible.  \nDiscarding T8: T8 unshown:4 left, so relatively safe.  \nDiscarding F2: F2 is prevailing wind, might be riskier because others might need it for wind pung. Unshown F2:3 left.  \nDiscarding B9: terminal, might be safer because many terminals discarded.  \n\nBut from hand improvement perspective, I should discard a tile that doesn't help my potential Mixed Straight. T3 and T8 are not needed for B789. F2 is not needed. Among them, T8 is a simple tile in a suit I already have a chow in, and it's not close to forming sequences with my other tiles (I have no T7 or T9). So T8 is a good discard.  \n\nAlternatively, discard T3, but T3 could potentially combine with T1-T2 (but across already has T1-2-3 chow, so T1 and T2 might be scarce).  \n\nI think discard T8 is reasonable.\n\nReason: I aim to develop a hand with potential for Mixed Straight (worth 8 points) by keeping B9 for a B7-B8-B9 chow and maintaining B2 and B4 pairs for future melds. Discarding T8 removes a tile 